llent disciple of Cicero?\par 3.\emdash  You are not a serious person, [since you] (}{\i\f211 who}{\f208 ) don't hesitate to talk trifles in Latin again and again.\par 4.\emdash  What [revolution] (}{\i\f211 new thing}{\f208 ) is [this]? Talking trifles in Latin is not allowed! There's no reason why we should make that language tedious.\par 5.\emdash  You use (}{\i\f211 by}{\f208 ) a dead language audaciously when you [speak about] (}{\i\f211 deal with}{\f208 ) modern things. Indeed, you dare to speak in Latin about coffee, or about the games that are called \ldblquote football\rdblquote  and \ldblquote rubgy\rdblquote  among the English. The ancient Romans did not know [about] all these things.\par 6.\emdash  Ancient customs differ from ours less than you [think] (}{\i\f211 believe}{\f208 ).\par 7. For example, the horses that draw our [cars] (}{\i\f211 carriages}{\f208 ) are steam-powered, but people always [go] (}{\i\f211 are transported}{\f208 ) by [cars] (}{\i\f211 carriages}{\f208 ).\par 8. The ancients played (}{\i\f211 by}{\f208 ) ball not entirely as we play today, but nevertheless they used balls \u8230\'c9\par 9. What do you think of this sentence: \ldblquote \u8230\'c9 he abandoned [horse-riding and fencing] (}{\i\f211 sports of horses and of arms}{\f208 ) and went over to [playing ball] (}{\i\f211 ball and large-inflated-ball}{\f208 )?\rdblquote .\par 10.\emdash  Suetonius wrote something similar concerning the Emperor Augustus.\par 11.\emdash  You see [that] the proverb \ldblquote nothing new under the sun\rdblquote  [does not lie] (}{\i\f211 not to lie}{\f208 ); and [that] Latin (}{\i\f211 language}{\f208 ), which you use today, [is not] dead (}{\i\f211 not to be}{\f208 ).\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Ab}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 tor}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 eris}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 ab}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 sus sum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to abuse}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 ab}{\f208  + u}{\b\f210 tor}{\f208 ). Do you know that the }{\i\f211 1}